{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","feature scaling","accuracy","tabular data"],"description":"The evidence primarily focuses on Z-score standardization as superior to other normalization methods (e.g., Min–Max) in improving KNN accuracy, but does not systematically compare the performance of standardization across different types of tabular data (e.g., continuous vs. mixed-type features, skewed vs. normally distributed features) or across varying feature scales (e.g., extreme outliers, very large/small ranges). This limits the generalizability of the findings to diverse real-world datasets.","evidence_ids":["E1"],"id":"G1","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"e031902f6ad86f1bc849bce16c362b48efe36074f3fccc55e8c6482977815ee3","kind":"artefact","prev":"9708f99a09ef268201eb72143ae9589538f4bf0202c0d21bc8eac9f7b82449f7","seq":25,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.015+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","hyperparameter optimization","KNN accuracy"],"description":"Only a few studies explicitly evaluate the interaction between standardization and specific KNN hyperparameters (e.g., k-value, distance metric, weighting scheme) across multiple datasets. For example, while E2 reports improved accuracy with optimized parameters and feature scaling, it does not investigate whether the gains are attributable specifically to standardization or to the combination of scaling and hyperparameter tuning.","evidence_ids":["E2"],"id":"G2","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"a4d0ad09f35972fa78bb1240905f5098d4944392fa52c4b30ec3442515dab373","kind":"artefact","prev":"e031902f6ad86f1bc849bce16c362b48efe36074f3fccc55e8c6482977815ee3","seq":26,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.015+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","missing data","class imbalance","feature selection"],"description":"There is no evidence on how standardization performs in the presence of missing data, class imbalance, or when combined with feature selection in tabular datasets. While E3 mentions feature selection alongside standardization, the impact of standardization on classification accuracy under such conditions remains untested.","evidence_ids":["E3"],"id":"G3","kind":"uncovered"},"hash":"2d38833684f242d9e3a83218dd8fcd682a5372be3bad4268a85ce3375d4f85ab","kind":"artefact","prev":"a4d0ad09f35972fa78bb1240905f5098d4944392fa52c4b30ec3442515dab373","seq":27,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.016+00:00"}
{"actor":"hypothesis","data":{"concepts":["standardization","cross-dataset validity","generalizability"],"description":"The claim that standardization improves accuracy is based on a limited number of datasets (e.g., date fruit, heart disease, credit scoring, indoor positioning), and none of the studies provide a meta-analysis or cross-dataset validation. This raises questions about whether the benefits of standardization are consistent across domains and data distributions.","evidence_ids":["E1","E3","E7","E5"],"id":"G4","kind":"narrow_scope"},"hash":"5bc86d2c1f8ba92e791de5776f4aee9b7a7e7fcfbb5160e87d88304ba4600920","kind":"artefact","prev":"2d38833684f242d9e3a83218dd8fcd682a5372be3bad4268a85ce3375d4f85ab","seq":28,"stage":"gaps","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.016+00:00"}
