{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer","digits_binary","wine_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H1","id":"D1","metrics":["accuracy"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on whether standardization improves KNN accuracy on tabular datasets with skewed or non-normally distributed features compared to Min–Max normalization. The control condition (knn) evaluates baseline performance without any feature scaling. The treatment conditions (knn+standardize and knn+min_max) directly compare standardization against Min–Max normalization. These three datasets are selected because: (1) they are standard, publicly available tabular datasets with real-world feature distributions (e.g., digits have high variance, wine has potentially skewed features), (2) they do not have known class imbalance (unlike the 'imb10' variants), so we isolate the effect of feature distribution skew on model performance; (3) they are small enough to allow meaningful cross-validation while being representative of typical tabular data. A paired Wilcoxon test is used to assess differences in accuracy between standardization and Min–Max, as it is appropriate for non-normal, paired observations. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and three random seeds ensure reproducibility and reduce variance due to randomness in data splitting.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize","knn+min_max"]},"hash":"f1c2a0897f3cb8723483f7bb79983c354b60cad6fddb4dfa9c6f07501db31871","kind":"artefact","prev":"831ff52f7bbbdf614e5766fe67acc3ea75861701ec4a72eaaa386b4fb8535c4c","seq":34,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.016+00:00"}
{"actor":"experiment","data":{"control":"knn","datasets":["breast_cancer","wine_binary","digits_binary"],"folds":5,"hypothesis_id":"H2","id":"D2","metrics":["accuracy"],"model":"knn","rationale":"The hypothesis focuses on the interaction between standardization and distance metric in KNN accuracy. To isolate this interaction, we use three datasets with varying feature scales and distributions—breast_cancer (30 features, moderate scale), wine_binary (13 features, small scale), and digits_binary (64 features, high dimensionality, potentially skewed). The control condition (knn) tests baseline performance without standardization. The treatment conditions introduce standardization and test it with Euclidean and Manhattan metrics specifically. Using paired Wilcoxon test allows comparison of accuracy between standardization and no-standardization within each metric, controlling for dataset variation. Five-fold cross-validation ensures robustness, and three seeds ensure reproducibility. Excluding imbalanced datasets (e.g., breast_cancer_imb10, digits_binary_imb10) avoids introducing class imbalance noise that could mask the distance metric–standardization interaction. This design directly tests the hypothesis: whether accuracy improves more with Euclidean than Manhattan when standardization is applied.","seeds":[123,456,789],"split":"stratified_kfold","test":"paired_wilcoxon","treatments":["knn+standardize","knn+standardize+euclidean","knn+standardize+manhattan"]},"hash":"e10cab45d378d538d40a3639bfbbb16f3d829408e93679cfcae2b4b22877cc15","kind":"artefact","prev":"f1c2a0897f3cb8723483f7bb79983c354b60cad6fddb4dfa9c6f07501db31871","seq":35,"stage":"design","ts":"2026-10-10T03:22:59.016+00:00"}
